.chapter 3
.h1 Printing with LaTeX [latex]
Strata writes a LaTeX file and a TeX engine makes the pages.  It has no
formatter and no printer drivers; the old program had both.
.h2 Decision [latex-reasons]
The design note of the Printer gives the reason.  The operating system reaches
printers through CUPS, so only the layout remains for the program.  A TeX
engine provides line breaking, pagination, fonts, footnotes, tables and
running heads.  Strata gets real pages, a real preview and a page count.  The
notes record why the engine changed:
- 10 November: the first design chose XeLaTeX, which reads UTF-8 and uses
  system fonts.  The machine of the author had pdfTeX only.
- 12 November: the Printer was written for pdfTeX with eight-bit fonts (T1
  encoding, Latin Modern), so the preamble needs no system font.  A character
  that no font has prints as {c:[U+XXXX]} and goes to the Error Log.
- The Preview needs a DVI file, which LaTeX writes and XeLaTeX does not.  One
  source therefore works with three routes: pdfTeX for the PDF, LaTeX for the
  DVI of the Viewer, and LaTeX with a converter for a PDF made from the DVI.
.h2 Printer [latex-how]
The Printer is a program of its own.  It loads a document, writes a LaTeX file
in a private temporary folder and runs pdfLaTeX there; for the other routes it
runs LaTeX, and dvipdfmx for a PDF made from the DVI.
.code
strprint FILE.DOC [-o OUT] [--dvi | --pdf-via-dvi]
                  [--contents LEVEL] [--index] [--list-figures]
                  [--list-tables] [--draft] [--double-spaced]
.end
The engine starts without shell escape, and the Printer escapes the text, so a
hostile document cannot make TeX run a command.  Only the user who started the
print can use the folder, and the Printer removes it at the end.  The exit
status is 0 only when the PDF exists; on failure the Printer prints the first
error line of the TeX log.
One macro sets every level of headline; LaTeX sections and counters are not
used.  The tag of a headline is the text that the Editor shows, so the printed
number cannot differ from the number on the screen.
The S of the logo was first a Metafont font.  A bitmap font needs a TeX
installation that can make it, travels with each document and needs code in
the Viewer; the first Preview showed empty boxes because the font files had
been removed.  A route through dvips could not print a normal S either.  On 15
November the project maintainer decided to draw the S with TikZ as a vector.
It is sharp at every size and needs no files and no Viewer code.
.h2 Passes [latex-settle]
A contents list, an index, a list of figures and a cross reference to a page
need the page numbers of the previous run.  The Printer runs the engine twice
when the document asks for such a part, and a third time only when the log of
the second run says that a label has moved.  Three is the limit, and a failed
pass is not repeated.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h3 Measurement [latex-passes]
The measurement of 18 November made structured documents of five sizes, from
10 to @pass_pages_last@ pages, with every feature that makes TeX run again:
contents of every level, index, lists of figures and tables, cross references
to sections and pages, a Section marker in the header, a footnote in every 25th
block and a long table.  {Tab:passes} gives the result.  Two passes settle
documents up to @max_pass_two@ pages; the largest size needs three.  A cross
reference without a target prints as two question marks.  The check of the
project reads each PDF as text and found none in any size, and no placeholder
of a missing section.  Every row of the contents and the index gives the page
of its headline.  The same check runs on the reference manual.
.tabcap passes Prints that need several passes
.table 8 9 11 18
Pages | Passes | Seconds | Peak memory (MB)
.datarows passes
.end
.pic pictures/story-print-time.png 4 Time of a print, by pages. [printtime]
.h2 Borders in print [latex-borders]
A LaTeX table draws vertical rules only between rows, so the first writer
printed double borders with gaps: the outer line floated above the corner and
the vertical lines broke between rows.  The test came first.  It reads a
printed page at 300 or 400 dpi and requires: unbroken
vertical lines from the first horizontal line to the last, also for the inner
line of a double line; a line between two columns through every line between
rows; horizontal lines from edge to edge.  The old writer failed eight checks.
The fix of 19 November:
1. Each cell draws the vertical lines of its own edges after its text, in its
padding, with a width of zero.  The table no longer uses LaTeX rules.
2. Each line between two rows is a row of its own, as high as the line, made
of one box per column and the vertical lines through it.
3. A line between two columns is drawn once, at the right edge of the left
column; the stronger kind wins on disagreement.
.endif
.h2 Hyphenation and templates [latex-templates]
A soft hyphen marks a place where a word may break; the Printer writes it as
{c:\-}.  The Maintain Dictionaries entry of the Document Manager keeps a
personal list of words with break places.  The Printer writes the list into
the preamble as {c:\hyphenation}.  The old program kept such lists in files of
its own; Strata reads them ({x:probing}).
A template gives a new document its named formats, page layout, header and
footer.  The New command lists the templates with a description of each.  The
shipped set has letters for US Letter and A4 and envelopes.  The A4 letter
puts the address where the window of a window envelope shows it.
